---
# Which checks run, and why:
# - bugprone-*     finds real mistakes (wrong argument order, dangling references, ...).
# - performance-*  finds needless copies (e.g. passing std::string by value).
# - readability-*  keeps the code easy to read.
# We do NOT enable modernize-* or cppcoreguidelines-*: they push "auto", trailing return types and
# other modern patterns that go against this project's plain coding style (see CONTRIBUTING.md).
#
# Checks turned off below:
# - readability-identifier-length / readability-magic-numbers: too noisy for short names like
#   "i" or obvious numbers like 200 (HTTP OK).
# - readability-use-anyofallof: suggests std::any_of with a lambda instead of a plain for loop.
# - readability-implicit-bool-conversion: flags normal checks such as "if (pointer)".
# - bugprone-easily-swappable-parameters: warns on almost every function that takes two
#   std::string parameters, which is common in an HTTP parser.
Checks: >
  -*,
  bugprone-*,
  performance-*,
  readability-*,
  -readability-identifier-length,
  -readability-magic-numbers,
  -readability-use-anyofallof,
  -readability-implicit-bool-conversion,
  -bugprone-easily-swappable-parameters
WarningsAsErrors: ''
# Also check our own headers (under src/), but not the vcpkg ones. [/\\] matches both "/" and the
# "\" that Windows paths use.
HeaderFilterRegex: '[/\\]src[/\\].*'
